{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ maps students spatial thinking learning)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#7_68554780","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3072","rank":1,"snippet":"difficult of these skills is the first. Asking geographic questions requires both an understanding of the key perspectives of geography and the knowledge and skills related to spatial thinking. It takes time, guidance, and practice to develop the ability to ask questions related to the “where” and the “how and why there” of a problem. It also takes repeated experiences in thinking spatially to become fluent with spatial concepts, to think in terms of patterns of objects in space (where), and to consider the processes that produce these patterns (how and why there). Maps are an essential tool to organize and display geographic information. Patterns and relationships among objects can become apparent on a map in a way that supports <cover>spatial thinking and problem solving. Thus learning to think geographically often leads students to a new appreciation for maps </cover>and the information they provide.\" , \"start_char\": 4496.000000 , \"title\": \"SA: Maps and Spatial Thinking Skills in the AP Human Geography Classroom | AP Central\" , \"url\": \"https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/courses/ap-human-geography/classroom-resources/maps-and-spatial-thinking-skills-classroom\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"18/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#7_68554780","relevance":3,"reason":"This document is dedicated to maps and spatial thinking skills in the classroom and directly explains pedagogical impact: maps organize and display geographic information, make patterns and relationships apparent, support spatial thinking and problem solving, and help students learn to think geographically.","learned":"Using maps can impact pedagogy by giving students repeated practice in spatial thinking, helping them see patterns and relationships, supporting problem solving, and developing geographic reasoning."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"This query produced a strong grade-3 classroom result on maps' impact on spatial thinking and learning, so continue the list for similar direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#8_68556814","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":2,"snippet":"takes time, guidance, and practice to develop the ability to ask questions related to the “where” and the “how and why there” of a problem. It also takes repeated experiences in thinking spatially to become fluent with spatial concepts, to think in terms of patterns of objects in space (where), and to consider the processes that produce these patterns (how and why there). Maps are an essential tool to organize and display geographic information. Patterns and relationships among objects can become apparent on a map in a way that supports <cover>spatial thinking and problem solving. Thus learning to think geographically often leads students to a new appreciation for maps </cover>and the information they provide. An important part of the AP ® Human Geography course involves using maps to learn significant content, to “think through maps,” as Liben explains it. ( Liben 2001, 76). However, human geography students must be critical consumers of maps and other spatial representations. “ Maps cannot be seen as separate from the contexts in which they are produced and used” (Morgan and Lambert 2004, 109). Just as texts are written by individuals with varying points of view and can be read and interpreted in different ways, maps are "}}
