{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1435566299#1_3074894720","relevance":3,"reason":"This document is dedicated to map skills for elementary students and directly supports the information need by describing classroom map activities tied to learning objectives and spatial thinking concepts, including large-format and tabletop maps for geography learning.","learned":"Using maps in pedagogy can target specific learning objectives and spatial-thinking concepts, introduce maps as representations of places, and support engaging geography learning for elementary students."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"The maps/spatial-thinking query is producing dedicated classroom map-pedagogy evidence, so continue to collect more grade-3 results from this productive result list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_20_1608936103#1_3530340518","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":4,"snippet":"Compass 2. Start with Small Maps 3. Teach them the Symbols 4. Get Them Outside Conclusion Author’s Bio \" , \"segment\": \"Students of today have grown with technology, so understandably, they are quite tech-savvy. Ask them for directions, and they’ll use the GPS on their device to help you. But, give some students big, geographical maps of us – and they’re completely lost. Technology might make many of the things we did obsolete, but <cover>spatial thinking is definitely not one of those things. Reading different types of maps goes beyond merely being able to get from one point to another on a daily basis. By learning how to read maps, students </cover>develop spatial reasoning skills that allow them to, literally – take their own place in this world. Ultimately, when a student develops spatial skills, they gain the capacity to understand the relations between symbols, pictures, objects, visualize them, or understand their relationship and distance. Without any doubt, learning to read a map helps students in building this important skill, the same as an efficient service that helps them build their research and writing skills. Tips to Help Students Build Mapping Skills Now that "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"21/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_20_1608936103#1_3530340518","relevance":3,"reason":"This document is dedicated to teaching students map-reading/mapping skills and directly explains pedagogical impact: reading maps develops spatial reasoning, helps students understand relationships among symbols, pictures, objects, and distances, and builds an important skill beyond simple navigation.","learned":"Map use can impact pedagogy by developing students' spatial reasoning and their ability to interpret relationships, symbols, locations, and distances."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"The maps/spatial-thinking result list is still producing dedicated and directly relevant classroom evidence, so continue to retrieve more potential grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1260126505#5_2526612927","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":5,"snippet":"of  Michigan  (Figure  2)  offers  a  more  accurate  representation  of  the  vote.7  In  this  way,  maps  and  graphics  may  play  a  role  in  legitimating  or  disputing  specific  ideologies,  beliefs,  and  practices. These  developments,  and  more,  both  allow  and  require  modern  citizens  to  understand   spatial   information   pre- sented  on  electronic  and  printed  maps  and  images. For  this  reason,  helping  students  become  competent  users  and  creators  of  these  technologies  should  be  an  important  element  of  all  of  the  social  studies—<cover>maps  are  not  just  for  geography  anymore. This  places  a  new—but  wel- comed—burden  on  geography  educators  to  ensure  that  map  learning  and  spatial  thinking  are  taught  and  taught  well  in  the  social  studies.8  But  are  they? Assessing  Students’ </cover>Knowledge Assessments   indicate   that   students   are  not   competent   map   users. An   analysis  of   the   2001   National   Assessment   of  Educational  Progress  (NAEP)  geography  exam  revealed  that  at  every  level  (grades  4,  8,  and  12)  test  items  that  required  stu- dents  to  use  and  interpret  maps  were  the  most  challenging  (see  Table  1). At  grade  4,   nine   of   the   ten   most   difficult   items  required   map   interpretation,   construc- tion,  or  use. For  grades  8  and  12,  five  and  seven  of  the  ten  most "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"22/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1260126505#5_2526612927","relevance":3,"reason":"The document directly addresses map learning and spatial thinking in social studies pedagogy. It argues that maps and graphics can legitimate or dispute ideologies, that modern citizens must understand spatial information on printed/electronic maps, and that educators should teach students to be competent users and creators of maps beyond geography.","learned":"Using maps can impact pedagogy by expanding social studies instruction beyond geography, fostering spatial literacy, critical interpretation of map representations, and student competence as map users and creators."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"The maps/spatial-thinking query continues to yield dedicated, directly relevant evidence about how map use shapes teaching and learning, so continue through the result list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1433042457#16_3069661559","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":6,"snippet":"1. Activate students’ prior knowledge. 2. Introduce the vocabulary term geographic information system (GIS). 3. Identify ways GIS impacts our everyday lives. 4. Have students create a shape, or polygon. 5. Have students create lines. 6. Have students create point s. 7. Query students. 8. Conduct a wrap-up discussion. Informal Assessment Tips & Modifications \" , \"segment\": \"21st Century Student Outcomes Information, Media, and Technology Skills Information, Communications, and Technology Literacy<cover>Learning and Innovation Skills Communication and Collaboration Geographic Skills Acquiring Geographic Information Organizing Geographic Information Connections to National Standards, Principles, and Practices National Geography Standards Standard 1 : How to use maps and other geographic representations, geospatial technologies, and spatial thinking to understand and communicate information ISTE Standards for Students (</cover>ISTE Standards*S) Standard 6 : Technology Operations and Concepts What You’ll Need Materials You Provide Rope (several different colors, including blue) Resources Provided The resources are also available at the top of the page. Required Technology Internet Access: Optional Tech Setup: 1 computer per classroom, Projector Physical Space Auditorium Classroom Community center Setup Large, open area Grouping Large-group instruction Other Notes This activity can also be used for professional development learning environments when introducing "}}
