{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1433042457#16_3069661559","relevance":3,"reason":"This appears to be a dedicated classroom GIS/map activity with learning outcomes and standards. It directly supports map-based pedagogy by connecting maps, geospatial technologies, and spatial thinking to understanding and communicating information, as well as communication, collaboration, and geographic skills.","learned":"GIS and map activities can shape pedagogy by developing geographic-information skills, spatial thinking, technology literacy, communication, collaboration, and understanding of how GIS affects everyday life."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"The current query is still yielding dedicated evidence about maps, spatial thinking, and classroom learning, so continue to find more grade-3 results within this productive list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1433177429#19_3069983975","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":7,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1433177429#19_3069983975\" , \"end_char\": 9626.000000 , \"headings\": \"Mapping the Classroom Mapping the Classroom Informal Assessment Extending the Learning Tips & Modifications \" , \"segment\": \"These include the classroom map outline, paper shapes for furniture, and sticky notes with students’ names. In Practice Find resources that show best teaching practices and example student outcomes for this activity. Picture of Practice Mapping the Classroom Background Information<cover>Learning to use and create maps builds students’ spatial thinking </cover>skills as well as language skills in the early years. Spatial thinking is important for students to develop as they connect the “why of where” in geography, Earth and environmental sciences, and history. Spatial thinking is also positively correlated with success in math and science, as it involves knowing and understanding spatial concepts and relations, how we represent those concepts and relations in different ways, and also how we can reason with spatial information. Students who acquire robust spatial thinking skills will be at an advantage in our increasingly global and technological society and will also build a foundation "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"24/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1433177429#19_3069983975","relevance":3,"reason":"This document is dedicated to a classroom mapping activity and directly explains pedagogical impact: learning to use and create maps builds spatial thinking and language skills, supports understanding geography, Earth/environmental science, and history, correlates with math and science success, and helps students reason with spatial information.","learned":"Classroom map-making can impact pedagogy by developing spatial thinking, language skills, cross-disciplinary reasoning, and foundations for math, science, geography, and technological literacy."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"The maps/spatial-thinking result list is highly productive for grade-3 evidence about classroom map use and pedagogical impact, so continue retrieving more results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1442714732#1_2935158164","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":8,"snippet":"maps improves visual literacy 3. Helps kids see past their own perspective How to teach kids about maps while getting them to draw their own map 1. Show your kids different levels of detail on maps 2. Measure distances on a map 3. Explore directions and connect them to distance 4. Understand what symbols mean 5. Map out the classroom Your kids CAN overcome difficult subjects in school – we believe it! \" , \"segment\": \"Learning about maps introduces spatial thinking<cover>Learning about maps helps children begin to understand what is going on in the spaces around them. According to National Geographic, “Spatial thinking is one of the most important skills that students </cover>can develop as they learn geography, Earth, and environmental sciences.” 2. Reading maps improves visual literacy According to learnnc.org, map skills contribute to the skill of reading images. This means that a student is comprehending an image, rather than just observing it. 3. Helps kids see past their own perspective Learning about where different cities and countries are located will help children gain a larger world view than what they see in their daily lives. If they can look at a globe and "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"25/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1442714732#1_2935158164","relevance":3,"reason":"This document is dedicated to teaching children about maps and directly explains pedagogical impacts: map learning introduces spatial thinking, improves visual literacy, helps students understand spaces around them, and broadens their perspective beyond daily experience.","learned":"Using maps can impact pedagogy by developing spatial thinking, visual literacy, image comprehension, distance/direction/symbol skills, and a broader world view."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"The maps/spatial-thinking query remains highly productive for dedicated classroom evidence on how map use affects learning, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1442714732#0_2935155987","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":9,"snippet":"useful while trying to find a new address, or when going on a road trip for older adults. Sure, but little kids don’t drive, right? So why should they learn to use maps, let alone make them? We will be exploring the benefits of young children learning how to make maps, and we’ll throw in some ideas of how to make that happen. Why maps are an important tool for a child’s learning development 1. Learning about maps introduces spatial thinking<cover>Learning about maps helps children begin to understand what is going on in the spaces around them. According to National Geographic, “Spatial thinking is one of the most important skills that students </cover>can develop as they learn geography, Earth, and environmental sciences.” 2. Reading maps improves visual literacy According to learnnc.org, map skills contribute to the skill of reading images. This means that a student is comprehending an image, rather than just observing it.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Teaching young children about maps for learning development\" , \"url\": \"https://www.schooliseasy.com/2016/02/teaching-young-children-"}}
