{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1442714732#0_2935155987","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a dedicated article on teaching young children about maps for learning development and directly answers the information need by explaining that maps introduce spatial thinking, help children understand surrounding spaces, and improve visual literacy/image comprehension. It repeats evidence from the same source as the previous result but remains directly relevant.","learned":"Teaching with maps can support young children's learning development by building spatial thinking and visual literacy, moving map use beyond navigation into core cognitive skills."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072","reason":"This result list is still producing grade-3 evidence about maps, spatial thinking, and classroom learning, so continue to retrieve more potentially relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1142831915#2_1916514910","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":10,"snippet":"READ PAPER What are the major curriculum issues? The use of mindmapping as a brainstorming exercise \" , \"segment\": \"Cognitive mapping gives us an opportunity to do this during the instruction process in training future leaders. A critical component of students' cognitive understanding is the negotiation among the many concepts and ideas they are continually processing (Ayersman, 1995). Graphical representations of the knowledge structures that exist in the <cover>students' cognitive understandings are called concept maps and concept maps (or \"cognitive maps\") are \"the spatial representation of ideas and their interrelationships that are stored in memory\" (Jonassen, 1993, as cited in Oughton andReed (2000). It is necessary to find out whether following a conscious and intense instruction process of cooperative learning and critical thinking </cover>certain conceptions and their interrelationships are established and how this is related to developing a vision and a problem solving strategy for the future. This is a constructivist view of concept mapping which involves a dynamic process and the use of mapping as an instructional tool. In this sense mind mapping is used to demonstrate and facilitate concept mapping process through technological contexts to create solutions for the existing and future curricular issues. "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"27/50"}}
