{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1432925355#10_3069346286","relevance":3,"reason":"This appears to be a dedicated geography classroom activity using maps/globes, and it directly supports the information need by tying map use to critical thinking, analysis, geographic-information skills, global awareness, and the standard of using maps/geospatial representations/spatial thinking to understand and communicate information.","learned":"Map-based classroom activities can impact pedagogy by supporting critical thinking, analysis, geographic-information acquisition, global awareness, hands-on learning, and spatial communication."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3077","reason":"The geography classroom/critical-thinking query produced a directly relevant map-pedagogy result, so continue this list for more evidence on maps supporting analysis and critical thinking in teaching."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3077"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#9_68558991","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3077","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#9_68558991\" , \"end_char\": 7013.000000 , \"headings\": \"Maps and Spatial Thinking Skills in the Classroom Maps and Spatial Thinking Skills in the <cover>Classroom On this page “Geography is the art of the mappable.” “The map speaks across the barriers of language.” Increased Interest in Maps Three contexts for spatial thinking Developing Geographic Skills 10 suggestions for developing skills as a geographer Thinking in Space Thinking About Space Thinking with Space References \" , \"segment\": \"An important part of the AP ® Human Geography course involves using maps to learn significant content, to “think through maps,” as Liben explains it. ( Liben 2001, 76). However, human geography students must be critical </cover>consumers of maps and other spatial representations. “ Maps cannot be seen as separate from the contexts in which they are produced and used” (Morgan and Lambert 2004, 109). Just as texts are written by individuals with varying points of view and can be read and interpreted in different ways, maps are not pure representations of reality but rather social productions subject to critical "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#9_68558991","relevance":3,"reason":"This document is dedicated to maps and spatial thinking in the classroom and directly explains pedagogical impact: students use maps to learn content and 'think through maps,' while also becoming critical consumers who interpret maps as contextual social productions rather than neutral reality.","learned":"Maps can impact pedagogy by supporting content learning, spatial reasoning, and critical literacy—students learn to analyze maps as constructed representations with perspectives and contexts."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3077","reason":"This query is yielding strong grade-3 evidence connecting classroom map use with spatial thinking and critical analysis, so continue to find additional relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3077"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#10_68561142","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3077","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_32828322#10_68561142\" , \"end_char\": 7716.000000 , \"headings\": \"Maps and Spatial Thinking Skills in the Classroom Maps and Spatial Thinking Skills in the <cover>Classroom On this page “Geography is the art of the mappable.” “The map speaks across the barriers of language.” Increased Interest in Maps Three contexts for spatial thinking Developing Geographic Skills 10 suggestions for developing skills as a geographer Thinking in Space Thinking About Space Thinking with Space References \" , \"segment\": \"When examining a map, the careful consumer should consider the conditions under which it was produced, whether it may be portraying a particular point of view, and what messages it may be conveying about power and perspective. It is important to develop a healthy, critical </cover>awareness and skepticism about maps as well as other graphics and images. As the French philosopher Henri Lefebvre (1901-1991) explained, maps and images both represent and reproduce space. Take, for example, the well-known 2004 election map showing red (Republican) and blue (Democratic) states. The map has taken on iconic status and "}}
